---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout>
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <a href="/writeups" class="text-sm text-cyan-500 hover:text-cyan-400 mb-8 inline-block">
      ← Back to Writeups
    </a>

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-2">PhishStrike</h1>
      <p class="text-slate-500 text-sm mb-4">
        CyberDefenders · Threat Intel · Phishing Analysis
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="text-xs bg-slate-800 text-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">Phishing</span>
        <span class="text-xs bg-slate-800 text-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">Email Analysis</span>
        <span class="text-xs bg-slate-800 text-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">Threat Intel</span>
        <span class="text-xs bg-slate-800 text-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">IOC Extraction</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Scenario -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Scenario</h2>
      <div class="bg-slate-900/50 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-6 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          As a cybersecurity analyst for an educational institution, you receive an alert about a phishing email targeting faculty members. 
          The email, appearing from a trusted contact, claims a $625,000 purchase and provides a link to download an invoice. 
          Your task is to investigate the email using Threat Intel tools, analyze the email headers, inspect the link for malicious content, 
          identify Indicators of Compromise (IOCs), and document findings to prevent potential fraud.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Investigation -->
    <section class="mb-14 space-y-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-2">Investigation</h2>

      <!-- Q1 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">1. Sender IP with SPF softfail + DKIM fail</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Analysis of the email headers shows the Authentication-Results section clearly listing an IP with SPF = softfail and DKIM = fail. 
          Legitimate emails should almost never have values other than “Pass”.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/a904f1eb-3e6a-4555-9143-e8375a442174.png" alt="Authentication Results" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q2 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">2. Return-Path</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          The <code class="text-cyan-400">Return-Path</code> header (envelope sender) was examined. Threat actors often set this to their own address so that bounce messages or replies are delivered to them.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_05_08-LinkedIn_SOC_Analyst_Strategy_-_Grok.png" alt="Return-Path header" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q3 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">3. IP hosting the malicious file</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          The email body is HTML. The malicious “invoice” link is contained inside an anchor tag and points to the distribution server.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_08_06-LinkedIn_SOC_Analyst_Strategy_-_Grok.png" alt="Malicious link in email body" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q4 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">4. Malware family (Cryptocurrency miner)</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          URLHaus shows the malicious URL distributes multiple malware families: <strong>BitRAT</strong>, <strong>AsyncRAT</strong>, and <strong>CoinMiner</strong>.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_17_50-URLhaus___http___107.175.247.199_loader_install.exe.png" alt="URLHaus report" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q5 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">5. URLs requested by the cryptocurrency malware</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Using the SHA-256 hash on VirusTotal → Relations tab reveals the URLs contacted by the sample.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_28_45-VirusTotal_-_File_-_453fb1c4b3b48361fa8a67dcedf1eaec39449cb5a146a7770c63d1dc0d75.png" alt="VirusTotal Relations" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q6 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">6. BitRAT persistence – Registry auto-run executable</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          VirusTotal Behavior → Registry actions shows the executable name written to the auto-run key.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_33_26-VirusTotal_-_File_-_bf7628695c2df7a3020034a065397592a1f8850e59f9a448b555bc1c8c63.png" alt="Registry actions" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q7 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">7. SHA-256 of the dropped autorun file</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          From the same VirusTotal report → Files Dropped section.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_38_55-VirusTotal_-_File_-_bf7628695c2df7a3020034a065397592a1f8850e59f9a448b555bc1c8c63.png" alt="Files Dropped" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q8 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">8. HTTP request used by the loader to retrieve BitRAT</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          VirusTotal Behavior → Network communications shows the exact HTTP request.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_44_04-VirusTotal_-_File_-_bf7628695c2df7a3020034a065397592a1f8850e59f9a448b555bc1c8c63.png" alt="HTTP requests" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q9 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">9. PowerShell delay (seconds)</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          The process tree shows a PowerShell process spawned with a base64-encoded command. Decoded with CyberChef.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_16_49_00-From_Base64_Remove_null_bytes_-_CyberChef.png" alt="CyberChef decode" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q10 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">10. BitRAT C2 domain</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          VirusTotal DNS section was incomplete. The any.run detonation report provided the clear C2 domain.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_17_03_04-Analysis_86c57967785fe8dbcdf209fb564f9a85.exe_(MD5__86C57967785FE8DBCDF209FB564F.png" alt="any.run C2" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>

      <!-- Q11 -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mb-3">11. AsyncRAT Telegram Bot ID</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          From MalwareBazaar → Hatching Triage report, the exact GET request to Telegram’s API reveals the Bot ID.
        </p>
        <img src="/images/phishstrike/2026-06-16_17_11_48-bitrat___5ca468704e7ccb8e1b37c0f7595c54df4e2f4035345b6e442e8bd4e11c58f791___Tria.png" alt="Telegram Bot ID" class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 w-full" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Footer note -->
    <section class="pt-8 border-t border-slate-800 text-sm text-slate-500">
      <p>Investigation performed as part of the CyberDefenders PhishStrike lab.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
